;; VT-d admission: may this IOMMU be programmed, and where is its IOTLB?
;;
;; `aiueos_vtd_initialize` in kernel/vtd.c opened with three decisions and then
;; never made another one -- everything after them is mechanism: allocating
;; pages, filling a second-level page table, writing RTADDR/GCMD/CCMD, spinning
;; on GSTS. vtd.c was the last kernel file with no decision moved out at all.
;;
;; The three, in the order the C made them:
;;
;;   1. TOPOLOGY. `drhd_count == 0` and `drhd_count != 1 || segment != 0` are
;;      two DIFFERENT outcomes and the C spelled them as two statements:
;;
;;        if (!aiueos_acpi_dmar_drhd_count()) return 1;              /* absent */
;;        if (drhd_count() != 1 || segment() != 0) return 0;         /* refuse */
;;
;;      No DMAR at all means there is no IOMMU on this machine, nothing to
;;      program, and a boot that succeeds. More than one remapping unit, or a
;;      unit on a PCI segment other than 0, means there IS an IOMMU and this
;;      kernel does not know how to cover it -- the rest of vtd.c programs
;;      exactly one unit and builds one context table for segment 0 bus 0, so
;;      admitting a second unit would leave its devices translating through a
;;      root table nobody wrote. Collapsing the two into one verdict would turn
;;      "no IOMMU" into a boot failure or, worse, "two IOMMUs" into a success.
;;
;;   2. CAPABILITY. `!version`, CAP bit 10, CAP bit 34, and ECAP bit 3:
;;
;;        if (!version || !(cap & (1ULL << (8 + 2))) || !(cap & (1ULL << 34)))
;;          return 0;
;;        interrupt_remapping_supported = !!(ecap & (1ULL << 3));
;;
;;      A zero VER register is not a version, it is an unmapped or dead window.
;;      CAP bit 10 is SAGAW bit 2 -- the 48-bit, 4-level adjusted guest address
;;      width -- and the second-level tables below are built with exactly four
;;      levels and AW=48 in every context entry, so a unit without it would be
;;      handed a page-table shape it cannot walk. CAP bit 34 is SLLPS bit 0,
;;      2 MiB second-level leaves, and `sl_pd` is filled with nothing else.
;;      ECAP bit 3 is not a refusal: it says whether interrupt remapping is
;;      available, and the caller allocates an IR table only if it is.
;;
;;   3. IOTLB OFFSET -- the security-relevant one:
;;
;;        uint32_t iotlb = (uint32_t)((ecap >> 8) & 0x3ffU) * 16U + 8U;
;;        if (iotlb < 8 || iotlb > 0xff8) { vtd_error = 5; return 0; }
;;        write64(iotlb, ...);
;;
;;      That offset is DERIVED from a hardware-reported ECAP field and is then
;;      used to address the 4 KiB MMIO register window. ECAP bits 17:8 are IRO,
;;      the IOTLB Register Offset: the VT-d specification puts the Invalidate
;;      Address register at IRO*16 and the IOTLB Invalidate register at
;;      IRO*16 + 8. IRO is ten bits, so the derived offset runs 8..16376, while
;;      the mapped window is one page: the widest 8-byte write that stays inside
;;      it starts at 0xff8 = 4088, which is why the bound is 0xff8 and not
;;      0x1000. Deriving an offset from untrusted input and bounding it is a
;;      judgement, not a register write -- the same shape as
;;      `net_tcp_peer_next` elsewhere in this tree. An unbounded IRO would put
;;      a 64-bit write with bits 63 and 60 set somewhere past the end of the
;;      device window, into whatever the page tables have next.
;;
;; THE PACKED PLAN
;;
;; One value, so the caller commits to a single admitted decision rather than
;; recombining an `interrupt_remapping_supported` decided against one ECAP with
;; an IOTLB offset decided against another. `aiueos-capability-plan` and
;; `aiueos-murakumo-join-plan` in this directory use the same shape.
;;
;;   0        REFUSE. Program nothing, fail the boot stage.
;;   bit 0    ADMITTED. Every clause held; the rest of this value is the plan.
;;   bit 1    DMAR ABSENT. No remapping hardware. Touch nothing and succeed.
;;            Mutually exclusive with bit 0 -- there is no unit to admit.
;;   bit 2    TOPOLOGY ADMITTED. Exactly one DRHD, on segment 0.
;;   bit 3    Interrupt remapping supported (ECAP.IR).
;;   bits 4.. The bounded IOTLB Invalidate register offset, 8..4088.
;;
;; Bits 3 and 4.. are meaningful only when bit 0 is set; the object still
;; computes them from the ECAP it was given, so a caller that reads them without
;; checking bit 0 gets values derived from that same ECAP rather than garbage.
;;
;; WHY BIT 2 EXISTS, AND WHY THE CALLER ASKS TWICE
;;
;; The topology decision is made from ACPI, before anything is mapped. The
;; capability and IOTLB decisions are made from CAP/ECAP, which are MMIO reads
;; that do not exist until the register window has been mapped -- and mapping it
;; is exactly what the topology decision authorises. So the caller asks twice:
;;
;;   gate = admit(drhd_count, segment, 0, 0, 0);      /* before mapping */
;;   if (gate & ABSENT)     return 1;
;;   if (!(gate & TOPOLOGY)) return 0;
;;   ... map the window, read VER/CAP/ECAP ...
;;   plan = admit(drhd_count, segment, version, cap, ecap);
;;   if (!(plan & ADMITTED)) return 0;
;;
;; The first call passes 0 for the three registers because they have not been
;; read yet, and 0 is not a sentinel: a zero VER genuinely is inadmissible, so
;; the first call returns bit 2 alone -- topology admitted, full admission
;; withheld -- which is the honest answer to "may I map this window?". The
;; second call re-decides the topology against the same ACPI values and
;; conjoins it into bit 0, so nothing the caller acts on was decided in the
;; first call and reused. Splitting the two phases into two objects, or
;; returning a bare 0/1 from each, is what would let them drift.
;;
;; WHAT MOVED, BEHAVIOURALLY
;;
;; The IOTLB bound is now checked BEFORE any register is written, where the C
;; checked it after RTADDR had been programmed, SRTP issued and a context-cache
;; invalidation completed. A machine that would have been refused now gets
;; refused without its root-table pointer being set. That is a change, and it is
;; the direction mmio-map-admit already moved paging.c: refuse before mutating,
;; not after. Its cost is that `vtd_error` 5 is no longer reachable -- the
;; refusal now shares code 1 with the version/CAP refusal that always used it.
;; main.c prints nothing for either (it names only codes 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9), so
;; no diagnostic is lost.
;;
;; SIGNEDNESS -- the C is unsigned, this object is signed i64
;;
;; `cap` and `ecap` are `uint64_t` register reads. A unit that reports bit 63 --
;; and the caller has no way to stop it -- arrives here as a NEGATIVE i64. This
;; is the one place in this object where a literal transcription of the C is
;; wrong, so each use is spelled out:
;;
;;   * BIT TESTS are sign-agnostic. `bit-and` is the machine's 64-bit AND, and
;;     every mask here is positive, so the result is in 0..mask and never
;;     negative however the sign bit of `cap` or `ecap` fell. `(> ... 0)` then
;;     means exactly "the bit is set", for any input.
;;
;;   * THE FIELD EXTRACTION IS NOT. There is no shift operator in this subset,
;;     so `(ecap >> 8)` has to be `quot` by 256 -- and `quot` truncates toward
;;     zero where `>>` floors. On a negative `ecap` the two disagree, and worse,
;;     the C masks AFTER shifting, which discards the sign-extended high bits
;;     that `quot` would have folded into the low ones. Written the obvious way,
;;     `(bit-and (quot ecap 256) 1023)`, an ECAP with bit 63 set yields an IRO
;;     that is not the one the hardware reported -- a wrong offset that can
;;     still pass the bound and address the wrong register.
;;
;;     So this object MASKS FIRST and divides second:
;;
;;       (quot (bit-and ecap 261888) 256)          261888 = 0x3ff00
;;
;;     `bit-and` clears bits 63..18 including the sign bit, so its result is a
;;     non-negative multiple of 256 in 0..261888, and on a non-negative multiple
;;     of 256 `quot` and `>>` agree exactly. The extracted IRO is then the same
;;     ten bits the C extracted, for every one of the 2^64 values ECAP can hold,
;;     sign bit included. Mask-then-divide is the general rule here: it is not
;;     an optimisation, it is what makes the division defined on the input.
;;
;;   * `drhd_count` (uint32_t), `segment` (uint16_t) and `version` (uint32_t)
;;     are all narrower than i64, so no value the C can produce arrives
;;     negative. They are compared for equality against 0 and 1 regardless, so a
;;     negative one would be refused rather than admitted.
;;
;;   * The derived offset cannot overflow: IRO is at most 1023, so `IRO*16 + 8`
;;     is at most 16376, and the packed result is at most `16376*16 + 15`, well
;;     under 2^19. The packing is a sum of disjoint fields, so `+` and `bit-or`
;;     agree on it.
;;
;;   * `(>= iotlb 8)` cannot fail -- the derivation adds 8 to a non-negative
;;     multiple of 16. It transcribes the C's `iotlb < 8` and is kept for the
;;     reason mmio-map-admit keeps its wraparound clause: it is a
;;     bound-consistency check that a future edit to the derivation would have
;;     to keep true, and the object should be total over its own signature
;;     rather than over the one line that happens to precede it today.
;;
;; FUEL -- no replenish, and none needed
;;
;; This object is deliberately absent from `bounded-memory?` in kotoba-native's
;; elf64.clj, so it gets no replenish and spends from its own context, which
;; starts at 512 for the whole boot. Measured from the emitted object, not
;; estimated: the disassembly contains exactly two `decq 0x8(%r9)` sites, one at
;; the entry of this function and one at the entry of `main`, which the kernel
;; never calls. There is no helper call and no recursion, so every invocation
;; costs exactly ONE unit on every path. `aiueos_vtd_initialize` invokes it
;; twice -- once before mapping, once after reading -- and main.c calls that
;; once, on the BSP only. The .data context is file-backed in the kernel image,
;; so the 512 is restored on every boot: 2 units spent of 512, a factor of 256
;; in hand. Adding a helper `defn` here would cost one more unit per call;
;; adding a walk would need a tier, which this deliberately does not have.

(defn aiueos-vtd-admit [drhd-count segment version cap ecap]
  (if (= drhd-count 0)
    ;; DMAR absent: no remapping hardware, nothing to program, and a success.
    ;; Bit 0 stays clear -- there is no unit to admit.
    2
    (if (and (= drhd-count 1) (= segment 0))
      (let [iro (quot (bit-and ecap 261888) 256)
            iotlb (+ (* iro 16) 8)
            remapping (if (> (bit-and ecap 8) 0) 1 0)]
        (if (and (not (= version 0))
                 (> (bit-and cap 1024) 0)
                 (> (bit-and cap 17179869184) 0)
                 (>= iotlb 8)
                 (<= iotlb 4088))
          ;; admitted | topology | remapping | offset
          (+ 5 (+ (* remapping 8) (* iotlb 16)))
          ;; Topology holds; the capability or offset clauses do not. The
          ;; pre-mapping caller reads this as "you may map the window", the
          ;; post-read caller as a refusal.
          4))
      0)))

(defn main [] 0)
